**Multi-cloud design.** Define a cloud-neutral interface, implement it once per vendor, and select the implementation by configuration. Patterns: **Strategy** (interchangeable implementations behind one interface), **Adapter** (each class adapts a vendor SDK to your interface) and **Factory** (creates the right one from config). If you need a whole family (storage plus queue plus secrets) per cloud, use **Abstract Factory**.

```java
interface ObjectStorage {
    void put(String key, byte[] data);
    byte[] get(String key);
    void delete(String key);
}
class S3Storage implements ObjectStorage {          // wraps AWS SDK
    public void put(String k, byte[] d) { /* s3Client.putObject(...) */ }
    public byte[] get(String k) { /* ... */ return null; }
    public void delete(String k) { /* ... */ }
}
class AzureBlobStorage implements ObjectStorage { /* wraps Azure SDK */ }

final class StorageFactory {
    static ObjectStorage create(String provider) {
        return switch (provider) {
            case "aws"   -> new S3Storage();
            case "azure" -> new AzureBlobStorage();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(provider);
        };
    }
}
```
Business code depends only on `ObjectStorage` (dependency inversion), and adding GCP means one new class (open/closed). Mention a common error model (translate vendor exceptions into your own), retries, and testing with a fake implementation.

**LRU cache whose values are lists.** Use a `HashMap` plus a doubly linked list for O(1) get and put, or `LinkedHashMap` with access order:

```java
class LRU<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, List<V>> {
    private final int cap;
    LRU(int cap) { super(16, 0.75f, true); this.cap = cap; }
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, List<V>> e) { return size() > cap; }
    void add(K key, V v) { computeIfAbsent(key, x -> new ArrayList<>()).add(v); }
}
```
Clarify the twist: does appending to a key's list count as a "use", and does capacity count keys or total elements? Mention thread safety (wrap or lock) and defensive copies of returned lists.
